A tooltip that overflows the window's right or bottom edge, and can't flip
to the other side of the pointer, lands at the left or top edge instead of
against the edge it overflowed.

The fallback shifts the tooltip back by its overflow, but subtracts the
window's right edge as well as the tooltip's: `x - right - window_right`
where it means `x - (right - window_right)`. That's always negative, so the
clamp to zero wins. In an 800 px window a 600 px tooltip at pointer x 500
can't flip (500 - 600 is past the left edge) and went to x 0, 500 px from
the pointer; it belongs at x 200, flush with the right edge. One wider than
the window still pins to zero, so its start stays readable. The same holds
for the bottom edge. The published 0.2.2 source has these lines; droppable
when a release fixes them.

Diffed against the tree with every earlier patch applied.

--- a/src/window.rs
+++ b/src/window.rs
@@ -2171,7 +2171,7 @@
                 } else {
                     tooltip_bounds.origin.x = cmp::max(
                         Pixels::ZERO,
-                        tooltip_bounds.origin.x - tooltip_bounds.right() - window_bounds.right(),
+                        tooltip_bounds.origin.x - (tooltip_bounds.right() - window_bounds.right()),
                     );
                 }
             }
@@ -2183,7 +2183,7 @@
                 } else {
                     tooltip_bounds.origin.y = cmp::max(
                         Pixels::ZERO,
-                        tooltip_bounds.origin.y - tooltip_bounds.bottom() - window_bounds.bottom(),
+                        tooltip_bounds.origin.y - (tooltip_bounds.bottom() - window_bounds.bottom()),
                     );
                 }
             }
